From: Abzu Project <build@abzu.local>
Subject: [ABZU 002] OpenBSD-style W^X enforcement knob for user mappings

Minimal patch #2: add a boot-arg / sysctl knob `abzu.wx_enforce` that makes
the BSD layer refuse mmap(PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC) and mprotect transitions into
W+X for unprivileged processes — mirroring OpenBSD's revocation semantics.
This is the kernel-side half of Abzu's "extremely secure" pillar; the
userland half lives in ../userland (OpenBSD libc already avoids W+X).

Rationale for keeping it *minimal*: we do not touch Mach IPC, zone, or SCHED
subsystems. One hook in vm_map entry protection promotion.

Index: xnu/bsd/kern/kern_mman.c
===================================================================
--- xnu.orig/bsd/kern/kern_mman.c
+++ xnu/bsd/kern/kern_mman.c
@@ -XX,X +XX,X @@
+#include <sys/systm.h>
+
+/* ABZU: OpenBSD-style W^X policy knob (see patches/README.md) */
+int abzu_wx_enforce = 1; /* default ON in Abzu builds */
+SYSCTL_INT(_security, OID_AUTO, abzu_wx, CTLFLAG_RW | CTLFLAG_KERNSEC,
+    &abzu_wx_enforce, 0, "Enforce non-writable-or-non-executable user mappings");
+
 sanity_check(
        vm_offset_t     start,  /* IN  */
        vm_size_t       size,   /* IN  */

